<p>Taiwanese President Lai Ching-te said Sunday that Taiwan would never be “sacrificed or traded away” and defended U.S. arms sales as necessary to regional peace. Lai was responding after President Trump said a pending weapons package for Taiwan “depends on China” and called it a negotiating chip after his summit with Chinese President Xi Jinping in Beijing. Lai said Taiwan would not give up sovereignty, dignity or its democratic way of life under pressure, and said there was no “Taiwan independence” issue because Taiwan is already a sovereign democratic state. Trump approved an $11 billion arms package for Taiwan in December that included missiles, drones, artillery systems and military software, but he has not approved a separate package reported at about $14 billion. The United States does not have formal diplomatic relations with Taiwan but remains its main arms supplier, while China claims Taiwan as part of its territory and has not ruled out using force.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/ZtIkim_YdVwsZu3ceHUp2NSh4_uDDgrrZ4Jv1GRBl2QL0A_ZKk_3xb6R6q-b7sEGy2PtPtYBBBXO5gKF8kyvS6I40NibthhoPPyrtugIgPZGNMejVOQwbvn7CoPorjX3KssEuOzP-kJDpx8dSlF9WgK0BR_B_Ytv74qll9wKOkK23mXbfgHXakkBDmtchI8fUGDcaoxWw1xdhedITwWYcV3e7FHPKgZfJAJMScXdNjUYFbVH39PSxxOefg' alt='Lai Ching-te says Taiwan will not give up its democratic and free way of life.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Summit focus: Trump characterized Taiwan as the most important issue for Xi and said it dominated discussions during the Beijing summit.</li><li>Trump’s caution: Trump said he did not want Taiwan to assume U.S. backing meant it could “go independent”.</li><li>Military pressure: Taiwanese officials accused China of intensifying military pressure through warships, fighter jets, large exercises and other coercive activity near the island.</li><li>U.S. sanctions idea: A Republican senator was reportedly preparing a proposal for immediate tariffs and economic sanctions if China invaded Taiwan.</li></ul><h3>Per